g back  back in but one of the things that's  really important is that the sort of  mental health of the country  needs things like music it needs  entertainment we need to be doing fun  things and  anything we can do to try and help that  is great so if we can keep the whole  thing going  until we get to that moment where we can  see the light at the end of the tunnel  and that's fantastic  uh and rick rob they're talking about  the mental health of the country uh but  also the  mental health of of roadies really  how have you been coping in that respect  um trying to keep out keep myself busy i  know a lot of  my friends and brothers and sisters at  the road have been doing the same  people have been looking for alternative  work  myself i found it very very difficult i  spent 40 years in the music industry  i don't really have uh any  qualifications for anybody anything else  really  um and i'm getting a little bit too old  to be  stuck in shelves in the supermarket  although i'd be happy to do that if  anybody would employ me to do it  um but we're all you know  i'm getting on uh all i do really is  travel the world with  rock and roll bands putting on rock and  roll shows and hopefully bringing  enjoyment to people yeah that's what i  want to get back  doing yeah no it's not it's not a bad  life mixing uh with some of the great  rock and roll red  we've heard you've had a wonderful life  as a roadie haven't you  i have i've worked with some great bands  or some good fantastic people  over the years from uh coldplay  iron maiden judas priest uh george  michael  spice girls i did top of the pops i've  done the brit awards the mtv  awards um we did the opening of the  channel tunnel  um greenwich millennium uh  net aid at wembley nelson mandela  that's enough name josh that's enough  name-dropping  you've impressed me you've impressed me  um so rob how much are you hoping to  to raise and how can people uh bid for  some of these wonderful items  well we've raised 